course: religion-ethics
lesson: 60
title: Accidents, wars and deaths on the news
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The news tells us about deaths from crashes, fires, wars and shipwrecks because they are terrible and unusual; most car journeys, school days and working days end safely, which is exactly why the rare sad ones become news.
explain: Picture Oscar in front of the TV: bombed buildings in Ukraine fill the screen and he asks, «Are people dying there right now?» His mum says yes, sadly, that he is safe at home, and that many people are helping; then she turns the news off and they talk.
explain: Real death is not like a game: in a game you respawn, but in real life a person who dies does not come back, and the people who loved them are left grieving.
explain: People can feel real sadness for strangers, like a famous footballer or an old queen they never met, and that is a different kind of sadness from losing family; rumours at the school gate spread fast and are often wrong, so we wait for what grown-ups actually say.
explain: Christians pray for people killed in accidents and war and for their families; many churches help refugees, remembering that Jesus said «I was a stranger and you welcomed me», and people of all faiths and none work for peace.
fact: Car crashes do happen, but most journeys end safely; seat belts, child seats and careful driving make them much safer.
fact: An accident is something nobody meant to happen; grown-ups can explain what happened simply, without upsetting details.
fact: In a war, soldiers and sometimes ordinary people, including children, are killed; it is one of the saddest things in the world.
fact: Russia began a full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022; many soldiers and civilians have been killed, and it was reported that millions had to leave their homes.
fact: Even fit young people can sometimes have hidden heart problems; it is rare, which is why it makes big news.
fact: People can grieve for a famous person they never met; when Queen Elizabeth II died in 2022, after 70 years as queen, on TV and on coins and stamps, she felt part of many people's lives, and crowds left flowers at royal palaces.
fact: People in small boats trying to reach safety in another country are sometimes drowned; rescuers save many, but not always all.
fact: Firefighters have a dangerous job, but they are trained and have special equipment, and almost all come home safely every day.
fact: In video games characters respawn; in real life a person who dies does not come back.
fact: Rumours spread fast after something terrible happens and are often wrong; it is better to wait for what school or family say.
fact: Railway lines, deep water and busy roads are dangerous places to play.
fact: Ukrainians often say «Вічная пам'ять», «memory eternal», for those who died; the Orthodox Church prays for the fallen and their families.
fact: It is fine to turn off frightening news and ask a grown-up to explain.
exercise: Ethan hears of a family killed in a crash and asks, «Could that happen to us?» What is honest? | Crashes do happen, but most journeys are safe, especially with seat belts | Every car journey is very likely to end in a crash of some kind | Crashes only ever happen to the families of bad, careless or very fast drivers | It could never happen to anyone he knows or to his own family
exercise: Daisy asks how her uncle died on the building site and if there was blood. What should grown-ups do? | Say simply what happened, honestly, without gory details | Show her photographs of the accident so that she understands it | Refuse to say anything about it, ever, even when she asks again | Make up a story that her uncle has gone abroad to work for a while
exercise: Was the building site death anyone's fault on purpose? | No, an accident is something nobody meant to happen | Yes, accidents are always done on purpose | Yes, it was Daisy's fault for not warning him | No, because building sites are never dangerous
exercise: Oscar sees bombed buildings in Ukraine on TV. Is that happening now? | Yes, sadly; people are being killed in the war, and many are helping | No, it is only an action film made with clever special effects and actors | No, wars like that only happened in the olden days, long ago | Yes, and it will very soon happen in Oscar's own street too
exercise: Taras says his cousin's dad was killed fighting. What is a good response? | Listen and say, «I'm really sorry» | Ask him how many enemies the dad killed | Tell him war is exciting like a film | Change the subject to football at once
exercise: A footballer dies of a heart attack at 34. How can a fit person die suddenly? | Rarely, fit people have hidden heart problems | Footballers run so much their hearts wear out at 30 | Only lazy people ever have heart attacks | It was certainly a made-up story online
exercise: Isla sees crowds leaving flowers after an old queen dies. Why cry for someone you never met? | She was part of their lives for many years, on TV and stamps | Everyone in the country was ordered by law to cry for a week | People were paid money by the palace to leave the flowers there | Only her family were really sad; the crowds were just pretending
exercise: Kai sees a boat sink with people trying to reach safety. Why didn't someone save them? | Rescuers saved some, but the sea was rough and help came too late for others | Nobody really cares about people in boats, so nobody tried to help | The people on the boat did not want to be saved by anyone | Boats never sink in the sea unless somebody sinks them on purpose, so it was a crime
exercise: Molly's dad is a firefighter. Will he die at work? | Firefighters face danger, but are trained and nearly all come home safely | Every firefighter dies in a fire one day; it is part of the job | Firefighters never go anywhere near real fires at all, only to cats in trees | Only firefighters who are scared ever get hurt at work
exercise: Noah says, «When you die, you just respawn.» What is true? | In games you come back; in real life a person who dies does not | Real people respawn after a few days | Respawning is just a long sleep | People respawn if they have extra lives
exercise: Kids whisper a teenager died messing about on the train tracks. What is wise? | Don't spread rumours; wait for what grown-ups actually say | Tell everyone the most exciting version of the story you have heard | Go down to the railway tracks to see where it happened for yourself | Text the whole story, with all the rumours, to the whole class at once
exercise: What does the teenager's death remind everyone about railway lines? | They are very dangerous and not places to play | They are safe if you are fast enough | They are only dangerous at night | They are safe on Sundays
exercise: What did Jesus say that leads many churches to help refugees? | «I was a stranger and you welcomed me» | «Stay in your own country for ever» | «Help only people you know well» | «Strangers must look after themselves»
exercise: What does «Вічная пам'ять», said by Ukrainians for the dead, mean? | Memory eternal | Rest in peace, soldier | Glory to the heroes | Happy birthday
exercise: Frightening news is on and Oscar feels scared. What can he do? | Turn it off and ask a grown-up to explain | Keep watching until he stops feeling scared | Watch it alone in his room at night | Pretend the news is not real
test: Noah, on his headset: «Bro, dying's mid, you just respawn, it's not that deep.» («Mid» means «nothing special»; «it's not that deep» means «it doesn't matter».) What is true? | Real people don't respawn; real death is for ever and hurts others | He is right; dying for real is just like dying in a game, so chill | People get three lives each, and then the game is over | Respawning is just how heaven works, after a short wait
test: What would happen if every car crash in the country was on the news? | The news would be full of them, but most journeys would still be safe | There would be no crashes left anywhere, because everyone would be scared | Every journey anyone made would be very dangerous indeed | Seat belts would stop working in cars all around the country
test: Ethan's grandad from Yorkshire says, «Buckle up, lad, an' tha'll be reet.» («Tha'll be reet» means «you'll be all right».) Is he right? | Yes, seat belts make journeys much safer | No, seat belts make crashes worse | Yes, seat belts make crashes impossible | No, buckling up is only for adults
test: Taras says, «His dad was a soldier and now he's dead. It's well grim.» His mate wants to say the perfect thing. What is enough? | «I'm really sorry» and listening | Nothing, it's better to stay away | «At least he died a hero, so be happy» | «My dad's a soldier in my game too»
test: An American kid says, «A footballer died at 34? That's sus, he was so fit, it must be fake.» What is the truth? | Rarely, fit people have hidden heart problems; it's real and sad | It must be fake, because really fit people never, ever die young | It's fake; the news always lies about famous people to get clicks | He died because he played too much football and wore his heart out
test: Isla's brother says, «Why's everyone crying? They didn't even know her, it's cringe.» Which reply is kindest and true? | Grief for a famous person is real, though different from losing family | He's right, and the people who cried should all be ashamed of themselves | People were told to cry by the King, so they had to do as they were told | They were only crying because all the shops were shut for the day of the funeral
test: A London kid says, «Allow it, the boat thing ain't our problem, innit.» («Allow it» means «forget it, leave it».) What is a fair answer? | It is sad, and many people of all faiths try to help refugees | He is right; nobody here needs to care about it at all | It is everybody's own fault for getting in the boat in the first place | Boats only ever sink like that in films, not in real life
test: Molly sobs, «Is Dad gonna die at work?» What should she do? | Ask Dad how firefighters stay safe | Ask Dad to never go to work again, ever | Hide Dad's uniform so that he can't go to work | Watch every single fire on the news, to check on him
test: At the gate, kids say, «He was messing on the tracks, everyone knows.» What is the wise move? | Don't spread it; rumours are often wrong, and remember tracks are dangerous | Pass it on, because everyone at the gate already knows anyway | Go down to the tracks after school to check it for yourself | Post it online with his name, so that other people learn from what he did wrong
test: The head teacher says, formally, «It is with profound sorrow that I must inform you of the death of a former pupil.» What does she mean? | A former pupil has died, and she is very sad | A former pupil has been expelled | A former pupil has won a prize | A former pupil is visiting the school
test: A cartoon shows a soldier hit by a cannonball who gets up laughing in the next scene. What is wrong with it? | Real war kills and hurts people, who do not get up again | Nothing, because soldiers in battles always get up again afterwards | Cartoons always show war exactly as it really is | Cannonballs only make people a bit dizzy for a minute
test: Daisy's cousin from Glasgow says, «Dinnae ask aboot the blood, hen, it's no' a game.» («Dinnae» means «don't»; «hen» is a friendly «dear».) Why might the cousin say that? | The details are hard, and grown-ups can explain simply without them | Daisy is not allowed to know anything at all about her uncle | There was a game being played on the building site that day | «Blood» is a rude swear word in Scotland, so she mustn't ever say it aloud
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the representation of fatal accidents in news reports? | Such events are reported because of their gravity and comparative rarity | Such events are reported because they occur on every journey | Such events are fabricated by broadcasters for entertainment | Such events are reported only when caused deliberately
exam: Identify the principal distinction between death as depicted in video games and death in reality. | In reality death is irreversible and causes grief to others | In reality the deceased returns after a brief interval | There exists no distinction between the two | In video games death is irreversible whereas in reality it is not
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the occupational risks of firefighters. | They are trained and equipped, and almost all return safely from their duties | They perish in the course of their duties without exception | They are never exposed to any danger | They are forbidden from approaching fires
exam: Ascertain the course of conduct most consistent with prudence upon hearing rumours of a death in the community. | To refrain from repetition and await reliable information | To disseminate the rumour as widely as possible | To visit the location of the incident | To embellish the rumour for the interest and entertainment of others
